          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em group object
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+category"} cartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalization
            for more on the general idea).
          %p
            In other words, a group object is something that behaves “just like” a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            but which need not have (just) an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
          %p
            For example, group objects in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+groups"} topological groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmthMfd"} SmthMfd
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Examples"} Examples
            below.
          %p
            Given a non-cartesian but
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
            one can still make sense of group objects in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+duality"} dual
            guise of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+monoids"} Hopf monoids
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#InABraidedMonoidalCategory"} further below
