              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverses"} inverses
          %p There is an alternative way to encode the specialization from monoid objects to group objects:
          %p
          #GroupsAsMonoidsWithCartesianAssociativity.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (G, m, \mathrm{e})
              can be made into a group object according to Def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GroupObjectInCartesianCategory"}
              iff its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associativity
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Associativity"} (1)
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+square"} cartesian
              (meaning: exhibiting a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              or
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              First assume that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
