                %path{:d => "M -2.484658 2.869271 C -2.033362 1.146296 -1.020294 0.333588 0.00106761 0.000783958 C -1.019331 -0.335326 -2.032306 -1.14765 -2.484602 -2.869129 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.47818;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(0.886975,0.456772,0.456772,-0.886975,139.697914,197.340051)"}
                %g{:style => "fill:rgb(0%,0%,0%);fill-opacity:1;"}
                  %use{:x => "81.118956", "xlink:href" => "#ibXJMASfORoMxJ2XOZ7Vr-ibn8Y=-glyph3-4", :y => "181.577546"}
                %path{:d => "M 104.549197 -43.71684 L 12.880506 -90.921478 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.47818;stroke-linecap:butt;stroke-linejoin:miter;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(0.997734,0,0,-0.997734,152.336186,106.515051)"}
                %path{:d => "M -2.487669 2.870626 C -2.03187 1.147368 -1.018889 0.33506 0.00150995 -0.00103422 C -1.019842 -0.333851 -2.032895 -1.146569 -2.487647 -2.867749 ", :style => "fill:none;stroke-width:0.47818;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke:rgb(0%,0%,0%);stroke-opacity:1;stroke-miterlimit:10;", :transform => "matrix(-0.886985,0.456762,0.456762,0.886985,164.974468,197.340071)"}
                %g{:style => "fill:rgb(0%,0%,0%);fill-opacity:1;"}
                  %use{:x => "214.212633", "xlink:href" => "#ibXJMASfORoMxJ2XOZ7Vr-ibn8Y=-glyph3-4", :y => "181.577546"}
            %p we need to show that there exists a unique dashed morphism making the left and right triangles commute.
            %p
              By alternative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
              to the two factors in the codomain
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \times G
              of these triangles, one immediately finds that the dashed morphism must be equal to both
            #eq:TheDashedMap.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (5)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi l
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi l
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi l
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mtext and
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi l
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msubsup
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\big(
                    l_2 ,\, m(l_2^{-1},l_1) ,\, r_1
                    \\big)
                    \\;\;\;
                    \\text{and}
                    \\;\;\;
                    \\big(
                    l_2 ,\, m(r_2,r_1^{-1}) ,\, r_1
                    \\big)
                    \\,,
            %p which is indeed consistent by the assumption that the solid diagram commutes, and using again the assumed inverses, since this says that:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi l
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi l
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi l
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi l
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msubsup
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    m(l_1, r_1) \;=\; m(l_2, r_2)
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    \\Leftrightarrow
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    m(l_2^{-1}, l_1) \;=\; m(r_2, r_1^{-1})
                    \\,.
            %p
              Conversely, assuming that the associativity square is Cartesian, we need to produce a consistent inverse-assigning map. To this end, specialize the maps in
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CartesianAssociativity"} (4)
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \,\coloneqq\, G
              and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi l
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} e
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi id
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mtext and
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi l
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi id
                    %mo ,
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} e
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (l_1, r_1) \coloneqq (\mathrm{e}\circ p, id)
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    \\text{and}
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    (l_2, r_2) \coloneqq (id, \mathrm{e}\circ p)
                    \\,,
            %p
              whence the dashed map
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:TheDashedMap"} (5)
              gives
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                        %mi id
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi id
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    G
                    \\xrightarrow{
                    \\;
                    \\big(
                    id ,\, (-)^{-1} ,\, id
                    \\big)
                    \\;
                    }
                    G \times G \times G
            %p
              from which we may project out the desired map
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^{-1}
          %h3#InABraidedMonoidalCategory In a braided monoidal category
          %p
            Notice (with Rem.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UseOfDiagonalMorphism"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal+maps"} diagonal maps
            in Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GroupObjectInCartesianCategory"}
            precludes direct generalization of this definition of group objects to non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
          %p
            Hence, while the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
            may generally be defined in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+elements"} inverse elements
            typically uses
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonoid+object"} comonoid object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            to substitute for the missing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal+maps"} diagonal maps
          %p
            Given this, inverses may be encoded by an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antipode"} antipode
            map and the resulting “monoidal group objects” are known as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+monoids"} Hopf monoids
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebras"} Hopf algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+groups"} quantum groups
          %p
            Hopf monoids may be defined in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
            is used in stating the fact that the comultiplication is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            of monoid objects.
          %h3#InTermsOfPresheavesOfGroups In terms of presheaves of groups
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              (in the sense of Def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GroupObjectInCartesianCategory"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              of the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Grp
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp^{C^{op}}
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{C^{op}}
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
          %p
            This is a special case of the general theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structures+in+presheaf+toposes"} structures in presheaf toposes
          %p
            It means that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Func
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Grp
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Func\big(\mathcal{C}^{op}, Grp\big)
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+category"} presheaf category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Func
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Func\big(\mathcal{C}^{op}, Set\big)
            (obtained by composing with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %p We unwind how this works:
          %p
            An object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            equipped with internal group structure is identified equivalently with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            of the form
          #eq:PresheafWithValuesInGroups.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (6)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Grp
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mrow
                                %mrow
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi G
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mo ⋅
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝒞
                            %mi op
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %mi y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi G
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mi Set
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; Grp
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \mathllap{{}^{(G,\cdot)}}\nearrow &amp; \big\downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathcal{C}^{op} &amp;\underset{y(G)}{\longrightarrow}&amp; Set
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^{op}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            is the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+homomorphisms"} group homomorphisms
            between them, and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            is the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            with
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maps"} maps
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            between them. Finally,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ↪
                            %mphantom
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mo −
                        %mtd
                          %mi PSh
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↦
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Hom
                            %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo ,
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  y \colon &amp; C &amp;\xhookrightarrow{\phantom{--}}&amp; PSh(C)
                  \\\
                  \&amp; G &amp;\mapsto&amp; Hom_C(-,G)
                  }
          %p
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            into its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi Func
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PSh(C) \,\coloneqq\, Func(C^{op}, Set)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+presheaf"} representable presheaf
            that it represents.
          %p
            Since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(S)
            (for
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \in \mathcal{C}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G(S) \coloneqq  y(G)(S) \coloneqq  Hom_C(S,G)
                  \\,.
          %p
            (This a also referred to as “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            seen at stage
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            Now, the lift
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:PresheafWithValuesInGroups"} (6)
            of such a presheaf of sets to a presheaf of groups equips for each object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \in \mathcal{C}
            the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(S) \coloneqq y(G)(S) \coloneqq Hom_C(S,G)
            with an ordinary group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} e
                      %mi S
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big(G(S), \cdot_S, \mathrm{e}_S\big)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi S
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdot_S
                  \\,\colon\,
                  G(S) \times G(S) \longrightarrow G(S)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Moreover, since morphisms in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+homomorphisms"} group homomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon S \to T
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            we get a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ×
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mo ⋅
                                %mi S
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↑
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mo
                                %mrow
                                  %mi G
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mo ×
                                  %mi G
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↑
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mo
                                %mrow
                                  %mi G
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ×
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mo ⋅
                                %mi T
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  G(S) \times G(S) &amp;\stackrel{\cdot_S}{\to}&amp; G(S)
                  \\\
                  \\big\uparrow\mathrlap{^{G(f)\times G(f)}}
                  \&amp;&amp; \big\uparrow\mathrlap{^{G(f)}}
                  \\\
                  G(T) \times G(T) &amp;\underset{\cdot_T}{\longrightarrow}&amp; G(T)
                  \\mathrlap{\,.}
                  }
          %p Taken together this means that there is a morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  y(G \times G)  \longrightarrow y(G)
          %p
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+presheaves"} representable presheaves
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            %em uniquely
            comes from a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot \colon G \times G \to G
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            that we are after.
          %p etc.
          %h3#AsDataStructure As data structure
          %p
            In the language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            (using the notation for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+pair+types"} dependent pair types
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+functions+and+dependent+pairs+--+table"} here
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GroupDataType-230121.jpg", :width => "740"}/
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TopologicalSpaces"} TopologicalSpaces
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SimplicialSets"} SimplicialSets
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%28Top%29"} Ho(Top)
                is an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H-group"} H-group
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SDiff"} SDiff
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergroup"} super Lie group
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
                (using the
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann-Hilton+argument"} Eckmann-Hilton argument
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
                is an abelian group again.
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                is a strict
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-group"} 2-group
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
                is a strict
                = succeed "-group" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CRing"} CRing
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^{op}
                is a commutative
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
            %li
              %p
                A group object in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+functor"} group functor
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+scheme"} group scheme
            %li
              %p
                A group object in an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cogroup+object"} cogroup object
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-sets"} G-sets
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-spaces"} G-spaces
                is a
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+group"} equivariant group
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semidirect+product+group"} semidirect product group
            %li
              %p
                A group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+stack"} group stack
          %h2#theory Theory
          %p
            The basic results of elementary group theory apply to group objects in any category with finite products. (Arguably, it is precisely the
            %em elementary
            results that apply in any such category.)
          %p
            The theory of group objects is an example of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"}> monoid
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} monoid object in an (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"}> group
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong group object
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} group object in an (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"}> group action
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+object"} action object
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"}> groupoid
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object"} groupoid object
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} groupoid object in an (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"}> infinity-groupoid
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid+object"} infinity-groupoid object
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} groupoid object in an (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"}> ring
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+object"} ring object
          %h2#References References
          %p The general definition of internal groups seems to have first been formulated in:
          %ul
            %li#Grothendieck61
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              , p. 104 (7 of 21) of:
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FGA"} FGA
              = succeed "," do
                %em Techniques de construction et théorèmes d’existence en géométrie algébrique III: préschémas quotients
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=SB_1960-1961__6__99_0"} numdam:SB_1960-1961__6__99_0
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/SB_1960-1961__6__99_0.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://translations.thosgood.com/fga/fga3.iii.xml"} web version
          %p
            following the general principle of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalization
            formulated in:
          %ul
            %li#Grothendieck60
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              , p. 340 (3 of 23) in:
